I am attaching two Excel workbooks:

1. COI_PM_UAT_Test_Cases.xlsx
   → This is the PM UAT workbook that needs to be refined.

2. Investment Funded Change UAT Excel
   → Use this as the REFERENCE/TEMPLATE for how the UAT test cases should be written, structured and presented.

Your task is to analyse BOTH workbooks carefully and then create a revised, final-quality COI PM UAT workbook.

IMPORTANT:
Do NOT simply copy the Investment workbook's test cases.
Use it to understand the expected writing style, level of detail, structure, granularity, terminology, formatting and way individual UAT scenarios are documented.

The PM workbook contains the intended COI PM coverage. Preserve and improve that coverage while making the test cases consistent with the Investment workbook.

==================================================
1. CORE REQUIREMENT
==================================================

Every individual validation must be ONE separate test case / ONE separate Excel row.

Do NOT combine multiple validations into a single summary test case.

For example, DO NOT write:

"Verify landing page fields"

Instead create separate test cases:

- Verify Case ID
- Verify Initiative Category
- Verify Project Name
- Verify Transaction
- Verify Rationale
- Verify Responsible Person
- Verify Accountable Executive
- Verify MT Sponsor
- Verify Business/Function
- Verify CFCR RFOs
- Verify Status
- Verify Created By
- Verify Created Date
- Verify Last Updated Date
- Verify Completed Date

Follow the same level of granularity used in the Investment workbook.

==================================================
2. USE THE INVESTMENT WORKBOOK AS THE WRITING TEMPLATE
==================================================

First analyse the Investment workbook and identify:

- How Test Case IDs are written
- How Test Scenarios are phrased
- How Test Steps are written
- How Expected Results are written
- Whether Preconditions/Test Data are used
- The level of detail expected in each row
- How fields are tested individually
- How positive and negative validations are represented
- How landing page cases are structured
- How workflow cases are structured
- How export cases are structured
- How common fields are handled
- How category-specific fields are handled
- How long-text fields are tested
- How hyperlinks/popups such as "Click to View" are tested
- Sheet naming and ordering
- Column ordering
- Formatting/style

Then apply that same approach consistently to the COI PM workbook.

The Investment workbook is the STYLE AND STRUCTURE REFERENCE, not a source for inventing COI requirements.

==================================================
3. DO NOT LOSE ANY PM COVERAGE
==================================================

The revised workbook must retain the PM coverage already present in the attached PM workbook.

The PM workbook should cover:

PM:
1. Initiative
2. Landing Page
3. Landing Page Export
4. Workflow
5. Workflow Export

Do not remove valid existing test cases merely to make the workbook shorter.

If the Investment workbook contains a better way of expressing an equivalent test case, rewrite the PM test case accordingly rather than deleting the coverage.

==================================================
4. COI CATEGORIES
==================================================

The PM workbook must cover ALL applicable COI categories, not just Corporate Action.

Include:

- New Product / Product Change
- Corporate Action
- NICRA / New Initiatives – Change Risk Assessment
- Other
- Investment Funded Change
- Non-Funded Change

Do NOT treat Corporate Action as the only example.

For category-specific testing, create individual test cases for the relevant fields of EACH category.

==================================================
5. INITIATIVE SHEET
==================================================

The Initiative sheet is PM-specific because PM initiates the assessment.

For every applicable category:

- Verify each field is displayed
- Verify field population/source where applicable
- Verify mandatory validation
- Verify configured field behaviour
- Verify selection behaviour
- Verify multiple selection behaviour where applicable
- Verify relevant character/format validation
- Verify submission
- Verify Save as Draft
- Verify Clear functionality where applicable

Use the exact field names from the BRD/reference material.

Do NOT invent fields.

==================================================
6. LANDING PAGE
==================================================

The Landing Page must contain individual test cases for:

A. Access
- PM access to COI landing page

B. Views
- My Cases
- All Cases
- Switching between My Cases and All Cases

C. Status tiles
Create individual test cases for each applicable status tile, including:
- In Progress
- Pending Endorsement
- Refer Back
- Endorsement by RFO
- Completed

Verify both:
- Tile visibility
- Correct count

D. Category selection
Create individual cases for every category.

E. Search
- Search with valid value
- Search with invalid/non-existing value
- Search behaviour

F. Filters
- Apply filter
- Filter result
- Clear filter

G. Sorting
- Ascending
- Descending

H. Pagination
- Pagination behaviour

I. Horizontal scrolling where applicable

==================================================
7. LANDING PAGE CATEGORY-SPECIFIC COLUMNS
==================================================

This is VERY IMPORTANT.

For EACH category, identify its actual landing-page fields from the BRD/reference material and create individual test cases for each field.

Do not write a generic:

"Verify category-specific columns."

Instead:

"Verify Project Name column"

"Verify Transaction column"

"Verify Rationale column"

etc.

Also include the common fields for every category:

- Case ID
- Status
- Created By
- Created Date
- Last Updated Date
- Completed Date

Use the exact field names wherever the BRD provides them.

==================================================
8. LONG TEXT / CLICK TO VIEW
==================================================

For fields such as:

- Transaction
- Rationale

where the UI displays a "Click to View" hyperlink/popup:

Create separate test cases for:

1. Verify the field is displayed as Click to View.
2. Verify selecting Click to View opens the popup.
3. Verify the complete underlying text is displayed.
4. Verify the text is not truncated.

Do not combine all of these into one generic test case.

==================================================
9. DETAILS PANEL
==================================================

Create individual test cases for the Details panel.

Verify:

- Details panel opens
- Correct case information is displayed
- Each relevant field is displayed individually
- Common fields are displayed
- Category-specific fields are displayed
- Status information is displayed
- RFO information is displayed
- Transaction/Rationale long descriptions can be opened through Click to View/popup where applicable
- Complete long text is displayed without truncation

Also include:

- Info tab
- History tab

with separate test cases.

==================================================
10. LANDING PAGE EXPORT
==================================================

Do NOT create one generic "Verify export" test case.

Create individual cases for:

- Export option available
- Export successfully generated/downloaded
- Exported file opens
- My Cases export
- All Cases export
- Category-specific export
- Filtered export
- Each exported field individually
- Common fields individually
- Long-text fields individually
- Exported data matching landing-page data

Every relevant field must have its own export validation.

==================================================
11. WORKFLOW
==================================================

Create individual test cases for:

- Opening assessment from landing page
- Workflow loading
- Workflow stages
- Current stage
- Current status
- Available PM actions
- Details panel
- Info tab
- History tab
- Relevant workflow fields
- Risk Assessment
- Mitigation Plan
- Submit for Endorsement
- RFO status
- Final endorsement/final submission
- Applicable validations

Each important field/action/validation should be an individual test case.

Do not combine unrelated validations.

==================================================
12. WORKFLOW EXPORT
==================================================

Create individual test cases for:

- Workflow Export option
- Export generation
- File download
- File opening
- Each exported workflow field
- Each relevant risk assessment response
- Mitigation information
- RFO/endorsement information
- Status
- Audit/common fields
- Long descriptions
- Data accuracy against the workflow UI

==================================================
13. EXPECTED RESULT WRITING STYLE
==================================================

Expected Results must be:

- Concise
- Clear
- Testable
- Specific
- Written as an observable system outcome

Do NOT write vague results such as:

"System works correctly."

Instead write:

"Project Name is displayed with the correct case value."

Or:

"CFCR RFOs are pre-populated from CRHS and the applicable RFOs are displayed."

Or:

"Selecting Click to View opens the popup displaying the complete Rationale text without truncation."

Do not put unnecessary explanations in Expected Result.

==================================================
14. TEST STEP WRITING STYLE
==================================================

Test Steps should also follow the Investment workbook's style.

Keep them actionable and sequential.

For example:

1. Log in as PM.
2. Navigate to COI.
3. Select Corporate Action.
4. Open the relevant assessment.
5. Review Project Name.

Do not write huge paragraphs inside Test Steps.

==================================================
15. BRD ACCURACY
==================================================

Use the BRD/reference information available in the attached material to determine:

- Exact field names
- Field source
- Population logic
- Mandatory/optional behaviour
- Character limits
- Dropdown/selection behaviour
- Multiple-selection rules
- PSID population
- CRHS/RFO population
- Category-specific behaviour

For example, the BRD confirms fields such as:

Corporate Action:
- Project Name
- Transaction
- Rationale
- Responsible Person
- Accountable Executive
- MT Sponsor
- Business/Function
- CFCR RFOs

NICRA:
- New Initiative Name
- New Initiative Summary
- First Line
- Senior Manager / Group Business Head
- Country Coverage
- Business/Function
- CFCR RFOs

Other:
- New Initiative Name
- New Initiative Summary
- First Line
- Approver
- Country Coverage
- Business/Function
- CFCR RFOs

New Product / Product Change:
- Programme code
- Programme Name
- Product manager
- Business head / Product head
- Business line
- CFCR RFO
- Product description & scope
- Applicable to: Islamic variant
- Applicable to: Sustainable finance variant

Use the actual BRD wording wherever available.

==================================================
16. IMPORTANT: DO NOT INVENT REQUIREMENTS
==================================================

If a field or behaviour is not supported by the BRD/reference workbook:

- Do not invent a requirement.
- Do not assume a validation.
- Do not manufacture field names.

Instead, use the available documented requirement.

Where the PM workbook currently contains a field whose exact BRD definition needs verification, retain it only if supported by the supplied reference material.

==================================================
17. TEST CASE ID CONSISTENCY
==================================================

Review the Investment workbook's ID convention.

Then make the PM IDs consistent in style and logical sequencing.

Every ID must represent exactly ONE test case.

No ranges.

No "LP-01 to LP-88" style grouping.

==================================================
18. FINAL WORKBOOK STRUCTURE
==================================================

Create a clean final PM workbook with separate sheets:

1. PM - Initiative
2. PM - Landing Page
3. PM - Landing Page Export
4. PM - Workflow
5. PM - Workflow Export

If the Investment workbook has a useful supporting/reference sheet structure, you may add a BRD/reference sheet, but do not add unnecessary sheets.

Keep the workbook professional and UAT-ready.

==================================================
19. QUALITY CHECK BEFORE FINALISING
==================================================

Before producing the final workbook, perform a consistency check:

- No duplicate test cases
- No summary rows pretending to be individual test cases
- Every row has one clear scenario
- Every scenario has clear steps
- Every scenario has one concise expected result
- Exact field names are used
- All categories are covered
- Common fields are covered
- Category-specific fields are covered
- Details panel is covered
- Long-text Click to View behaviour is covered
- Landing Page Export is covered
- Workflow is covered
- Workflow Export is covered
- PM-specific functionality is retained
- Test case IDs are sequential and consistent
- Writing style matches the Investment workbook
- Formatting matches the Investment workbook as closely as practical

Most importantly:

The final workbook should look like it was created by the SAME person/team who created the Investment Funded Change UAT workbook, while containing the complete COI PM requirements.

Do not give me a high-level summary instead of the workbook. Create the revised Excel workbook with all individual test cases as rows.
